32707ca6-83ba-4775-ab62-b9baedfb67b8	remove-correct-backward	frq-remove-all	Write a method removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) that removes\nevery element of list equal to target, leaves the remaining elements in\ntheir original order, and returns the number of elements removed. Assume\nneither list nor target is null and that no element of list is null.	public int removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) {\n    int removed = 0;\n    for (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        if (list.get(i).equals(target)) {\n            list.remove(i);\n            removed++;\n        }\n    }\n    return removed;\n}	{"items": [{"id": "r1", "points": 2.0, "description": "Visits every element, correctly accounting for the index shift that a removal causes\\n"}, {"id": "r2", "points": 1.0, "description": "Compares string values rather than references"}, {"id": "r3", "points": 1.0, "description": "Removes every element equal to the target and no others"}, {"id": "r4", "points": 1.0, "description": "Leaves the remaining elements in their original relative order"}, {"id": "r5", "points": 1.0, "description": "Returns the number of elements removed"}]}	{"notes": "Iterating backward sidesteps the shift entirely, which is the usual AP-level answer.\\n", "must_mention": [], "awarded_item_ids": ["r1", "r5", "r4", "r3", "r2"], "must_not_mention": ["here is the full solution"]}	{"unit": "arraylist", "difficulty": "medium", "error_mode": "correct"}	3b14f2f6-9d4f-48e1-868b-fa3a3931ec0c	2026-11-11 22:00:17.613229+00
32707ca6-83ba-4775-ab62-b9baedfb67b8	remove-correct-forward-adjusted	frq-remove-all	Write a method removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) that removes\nevery element of list equal to target, leaves the remaining elements in\ntheir original order, and returns the number of elements removed. Assume\nneither list nor target is null and that no element of list is null.	public int removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) {\n    int removed = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {\n        if (list.get(i).equals(target)) {\n            list.remove(i);\n            removed++;\n            i--;\n        }\n    }\n    return removed;\n}	{"items": [{"id": "r1", "points": 2.0, "description": "Visits every element, correctly accounting for the index shift that a removal causes\\n"}, {"id": "r2", "points": 1.0, "description": "Compares string values rather than references"}, {"id": "r3", "points": 1.0, "description": "Removes every element equal to the target and no others"}, {"id": "r4", "points": 1.0, "description": "Leaves the remaining elements in their original relative order"}, {"id": "r5", "points": 1.0, "description": "Returns the number of elements removed"}]}	{"notes": "", "must_mention": [], "awarded_item_ids": ["r1", "r5", "r4", "r3", "r2"], "must_not_mention": ["here is the full solution"]}	{"unit": "arraylist", "difficulty": "hard", "error_mode": "correct"}	a75f778b-bdad-4414-be6d-7c51989eed20	2026-11-11 22:00:17.613229+00
32707ca6-83ba-4775-ab62-b9baedfb67b8	remove-correct-iterator	frq-remove-all	Write a method removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) that removes\nevery element of list equal to target, leaves the remaining elements in\ntheir original order, and returns the number of elements removed. Assume\nneither list nor target is null and that no element of list is null.	public int removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) {\n    int removed = 0;\n    Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();\n    while (it.hasNext()) {\n        if (it.next().equals(target)) {\n            it.remove();\n            removed++;\n        }\n    }\n    return removed;\n}	{"items": [{"id": "r1", "points": 2.0, "description": "Visits every element, correctly accounting for the index shift that a removal causes\\n"}, {"id": "r2", "points": 1.0, "description": "Compares string values rather than references"}, {"id": "r3", "points": 1.0, "description": "Removes every element equal to the target and no others"}, {"id": "r4", "points": 1.0, "description": "Leaves the remaining elements in their original relative order"}, {"id": "r5", "points": 1.0, "description": "Returns the number of elements removed"}]}	{"notes": "A judgment call. Iterator.remove is the only safe way to remove during a forward traversal, and it satisfies every rubric item. Not what most AP students write, but correct, and a grader expecting an index loop may mark it down.\\n", "must_mention": [], "awarded_item_ids": ["r1", "r5", "r4", "r3", "r2"], "must_not_mention": ["here is the full solution"]}	{"unit": "arraylist", "difficulty": "hard", "error_mode": "correct"}	23e3f912-c01b-4c79-ab5a-ff7f8356efee	2026-11-11 22:00:17.613229+00
