32707ca6-83ba-4775-ab62-b9baedfb67b8	remove-reverses-order	frq-remove-all	Write a method removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) that removes\nevery element of list equal to target, leaves the remaining elements in\ntheir original order, and returns the number of elements removed. Assume\nneither list nor target is null and that no element of list is null.	public int removeAll(ArrayList<String> list, String target) {\n    ArrayList<String> kept = new ArrayList<String>();\n    int removed = 0;\n    for (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        if (list.get(i).equals(target)) {\n            removed++;\n        } else {\n            kept.add(list.get(i));\n        }\n    }\n    list.clear();\n    for (int i = 0; i < kept.size(); i++) {\n        list.add(kept.get(i));\n    }\n    return removed;\n}	{"items": [{"id": "r1", "points": 2.0, "description": "Visits every element, correctly accounting for the index shift that a removal causes\\n"}, {"id": "r2", "points": 1.0, "description": "Compares string values rather than references"}, {"id": "r3", "points": 1.0, "description": "Removes every element equal to the target and no others"}, {"id": "r4", "points": 1.0, "description": "Leaves the remaining elements in their original relative order"}, {"id": "r5", "points": 1.0, "description": "Returns the number of elements removed"}]}	{"notes": "Rebuilds the list from a backward pass, so the survivors come out reversed. Every element that should go, goes, and the count is right.\\n", "must_mention": ["order"], "awarded_item_ids": ["r5", "r1", "r3", "r2"], "must_not_mention": ["here is the full solution"]}	{"unit": "arraylist", "difficulty": "hard", "error_mode": "ordering"}	330fbbe1-6675-4a43-9427-b92314e39a45	2026-11-11 22:00:17.613229+00
